  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nWe propose a refined gradient-based optimization approach with adaptive learning rates and improved constraint handling to minimize C\u2085. The key improvements include:\n\n- Using initial_h_values as the starting point to leverage existing progress.\n- Implementing an efficient constraint repair mechanism to maintain feasibility.\n- Introducing a structured initialization when initial_h_values is unavailable.\n- Employing a dynamic learning rate adaptation based on progress.\n- Using a non-linear search strategy for better exploration.\n\nThe approach balances local gradient information with global exploration to avoid local minima, particularly useful given the non-convex nature of the problem.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nDefine the problem:\nMinimize C\u2085 = max\u2096 \u222bh(x)(1 - h(x+k)) dx\nSubject to:\n0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1, \u2200x \u2208 [0,2]\n\u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x) dx = 1\n\nVariables: h = [h\u2080, h\u2081, ..., h_{n-1}] where n = n_points\n\nThe objective is non-convex due to the h(x)(1 - h(x+k)) term.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\na. Initialization:\nIf initial_h_values exists:\n  - Interpolate to match n_points.\n  - Repair to ensure sum(h) = n / 2 and 0 \u2264 h \u2264 1.\nElse:\n  - Initialize h with a triangular pulse, peaks at x=1, widths determined by expert knowledge.\n\nb. Gradient computation:\nCompute gradient \u2207C\u2085 using central finite differences with step \u03b5.\n\nc. Optimization loop:\nFor each iteration:\n  Compute current C\u2085 and gradients.\n  Update h: h_new = h - learning_rate * gradient.\n  Repair h_new to maintain constraints.\n  Compute new C\u2085. If improved, keep; else, apply simulated annealing with adaptive temperature.\n\nd. Learning rate adaptation:\nDecrease learning_rate by 0.95 every 100 iterations without improvement, down to a minimum of 1e-6.\n\ne. Temperature schedule:\nAnnealing temperature starts at 0.1 and decreases by 0.99 every 100 iterations.\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n- Initial triangular pulse shape with adaptive widths.\n- Learning rate start: 0.01, decay factor 0.95 every 100 steps.\n- Simulated annealing acceptance probability: exp(-(\u0394C\u2085)/T).\n- Maximum iterations: 50,000, with budget-aware termination.\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\nEstimated computational effort:\n- Gradient computation: O(n\u00b2) per iteration.\n- Learning rate adaptive mechanism: O(1).\n\nWith n=100 and 50k iterations, this approach should complete within 600 seconds on a single CPU.\n\n6. Validation and return contract\nEnsure:\n- All h[i] \u2208 [0,1].\n- Sum(h) = n / 2 within 1e-8 tolerance.\n- Return (h_best, C\u2085_best, n_points) with feasibility validated.",
